BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surf-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sanford Underground Research Facility: Artist-in-Residenc
 e Program 2027 (Lead\, South Dakota)
DESCRIPTION:The SURF Artist-in-Residence program invites artists to create 
 work inspired by the Sanford Underground Research Facility in Lead\, South
  Dakota. At 4\,850 feet deep\, SURF is the deepest underground laboratory 
 in the United States\, housed in the former Homestake Gold Mine\, and host
 s experiments in physics\, biology\, geology and engineering. The residenc
 y is modeled after programs run by the National Park Service\, CERN and Fe
 rmilab\, and aims to create awareness of the science and encourage interdi
 sciplinary work. STRUCTURE: a four-week on-site visit in summer 2027 (betw
 een 22 May and 31 July\, timed to the artist's schedule) with studio time\
 , guided underground visits\, tours of Lead and the surrounding Black Hill
 s sites\, and dinners with scientists\; then a one-week visit in October 2
 027 to install an art exhibition\, give up to two lectures based on the pr
 oposal\, and run promotional or outreach events in the local community (wh
 ich may include schools\, universities\, interviews or documentation of th
 e residency). FUNDING: a USD 5\,000 stipend paid in two installments\; the
  artist may apply for up to USD 5\,000 in additional funds for creating th
 e artwork\; transportation\, housing and per diem for both visits\; and re
 turn shipping covered up to USD 7\,000. CONDITIONS: each artist donates at
  least one artwork\, selected by a SURF representative\, to SURF's permane
 nt art collection\, and is responsible for installation and de-installatio
 n in coordination with SURF and the exhibition space. A timeline and expec
 tations are agreed in an MOU before the residency begins. PHYSICAL REQUIRE
 MENTS: working underground requires being comfortable in dark spaces\, fol
 lowing all safety protocols\, walking on uneven surfaces for at least a mi
 le at a time\, and negotiating wet\, slippery conditions and an elevator r
 ide in the dark\; safety training and loaned personal protective equipment
  are mandatory\, and a SURF guide accompanies the artist at all times. DEA
 DLINE: 1 November 2026\, via the SURF AiR application portal.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Sanford Underground Research Facility (SURF)\nLocation: Sanford Under
 ground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\, in the Black Hills.
  The call does not state a residency or nationality restriction\; travel\,
  housing and per diem for both visits are covered.\nAward: USD 5\,000 stip
 end paid in two installments\, plus up to USD 5\,000 in additional funds t
 he artist may apply for to create the artwork. Transportation (airfare\, r
 ental car or mileage)\, housing and per diem are covered for both visits\;
  a studio space and guided surface and underground tours are provided\; re
 turn shipping of the exhibition is covered up to USD 7\,000.\nDuration: Fo
 ur-week on-site residency in summer 2027 (scheduled between 22 May and 31 
 July in coordination with SURF)\, plus a one-week exhibition installation 
 and guest-lecture visit in October 2027.\n\nMore: https://sanfordlab.org/p
 rograms/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sanfordlab.org/programs/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sanford Underground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\
 , in the Black Hills. The call does not state a residency or nationality r
 estriction\; travel\, housing and per diem for both visits are covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:humanity-ai-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Humanity AI: Open Call for Proposals 2026
DESCRIPTION:Humanity AI\, an initiative of ten leading philanthropies\, ope
 ns a USD 10 million call for community-led initiatives that shape how AI a
 ffects people's lives. ISSUE AREAS: Education (student and educator-center
 ed work shaping AI's educational impact)\; Labor and Economy (worker-led e
 fforts to ensure AI enhances rather than replaces employment)\; and Humani
 ties\, Arts\, and Culture (artist and creator-focused work protecting cult
 ural contributions). ELIGIBLE APPROACHES: shaping the rules for AI governa
 nce and deployment\; building AI controlled by communities rather than cor
 porations\; and challenging misaligned AI deployments. Community-led means
  the affected community defines the goals and shapes the approach. Priorit
 y goes to open-weight and open-source AI models over proprietary ones. OUT
  OF SCOPE: AI literacy training\, AI adoption promotion\, and research-onl
 y work. GRANTS: three tiers from USD 75\,000 to 1\,000\,000\, up to three 
 years. ELIGIBILITY: US-based 501(c)(3) organizations as lead applicants\; 
 individuals via fiscal sponsors or collaborations\; Tribal governments tre
 ated as States under IRC Section 7871\; for-profit entities only as collab
 orators\, not leads\; all proposals must serve US-based communities. TIMEL
 INE: launched 10 September 2026\; applicant webinar 14 September 2026\; in
 itial applications due 21 October 2026 at 4:59pm PT\; finalists notified D
 ecember 2026\; awards announced early spring 2027. Apply via Submittable\;
  an organizational readiness tool on the site helps assess eligibility fir
 st.\n\nOrganisation: Humanity AI (coalition of 10 philanthropies)\nLocatio
 n: United States. Lead applicants must be US-based 501(c)(3) organizations
  (or Tribal governments)\; all proposals must serve US-based communities. 
 Individuals can apply through a fiscal sponsor or as part of a collaborati
 on.\nAward: USD 10 million total pool\, grants of USD 75\,000 to 1\,000\,0
 00 across three tiers: Tier 1 USD 75\,000-150\,000\; Tier 2 USD 150\,000-5
 00\,000\; Tier 3 USD 500\,000-1\,000\,000. Grants run up to three years. N
 o application fee.\nDuration: Up to three years\n\nMore: https://humanitya
 i.ai/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://humanityai.ai/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Lead applicants must be US-based 501(c)(3) organiza
 tions (or Tribal governments)\; all proposals must serve US-based communit
 ies. Individuals can apply through a fiscal sponsor or as part of a collab
 oration.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:simons-foundation-open-interval-turbulence-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Interval: An Artist-Scientist Exploration of Turbule
 nce
DESCRIPTION:Open Interval is a Simons Foundation program that supports trio
 s consisting of an artist\, a scientist\, and a representative from a 501(
 c)(3) arts organization to collaborate around a shared scientific theme. T
 he current cycle centers on the theme of turbulence\, a phenomenon at the 
 edge of chaos and beauty. Awarded trios receive USD 30\,000 to support a s
 ustained period of thought partnership and exploration rather than the pro
 duction of finished works or exhibitions. The program runs from April to D
 ecember 2027 with a touchstone meeting in May 2027 and possible cohort con
 venings. Trio members must be regionally co-located anywhere in the United
  States so they can collaborate in person. The scientist should hold a Ph.
 D. and be conducting basic research\, with priority given to fields such a
 s astrophysics\, neuroscience\, quantum physics\, biology\, or mathematics
 . The application portal opens September 9\, 2026\, and all three trio mem
 bers must submit for the application to be complete.\n\nOrganisation: Simo
 ns Foundation\nLocation: United States (trio must be regionally co-located
 )\nAward: USD 30\,000 per awarded trio\nDuration: April to December 2027 (
 several months of collaboration)\n\nMore: https://www.simonsfoundation.org
 /2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-an-artist-scientist-exploration-of-t
 urbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-a
 n-artist-scientist-exploration-of-turbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (trio must be regionally co-located)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harvard-radcliffe-institute-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harvard Radcliffe Institute: Fellowship Program 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Harvard Radcliffe Institute Fellowship offers scientists\, 
 writers\, scholars\, public intellectuals\, and artists a year to pursue a
 mbitious projects in a vibrant interdisciplinary setting amid the resource
 s of Harvard University. The Institute welcomes innovative work that confr
 onts pressing social\, scientific\, and policy issues and seeks to engage 
 audiences beyond academia. Reflecting Radcliffe's history\, it welcomes (b
 ut does not limit eligibility to) proposals focused on women\, gender\, an
 d society\, or that draw on the Schlesinger Library's collections. It also
  invites proposals relevant to the Institute's 2024-2029 focus area\, acad
 emic freedom and connecting across difference (intellectual virtues\, free
  and open inquiry\, diversity of thought\, political polarization\, peace 
 and conflict\, inequality\, religious pluralism\, and related higher-educa
 tion policy issues)\, including work that constructively challenges discip
 linary orthodoxies or advances transformative perspectives. SUPPORT: USD 7
 8\,000 stipend plus USD 5\,000 for project expenses\, with relocation\, ho
 using\, and childcare funds available\; fellows must reside in the Cambrid
 ge/Boston area from September through May. ELIGIBILITY: open to individual
 s across career stages\; tenure is not required and applicants need not be
  academics. This is NOT a postdoctoral fellowship\; those currently enroll
 ed in a degree program are ineligible\, as are former Harvard Radcliffe fe
 llows (1999-present). Applicants must meet discipline-specific criteria. A
 PPLY: register on the online portal and select an area (Humanities and Soc
 ial Sciences\; Creative Arts\; Nonfiction and Journalism\; or Science\, En
 gineering\, and Mathematics). Materials: application form\, CV\, 1\,400-wo
 rd project proposal (with bibliography when appropriate)\, a writing or wo
 rk sample\, and three references. DEADLINES: humanities\, social sciences\
 , creative arts\, and nonfiction and journalism by 10 September 2026\, 5pm
  ET\; science\, engineering\, and mathematics by 1 October 2026\, 5pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Harvard Radcliffe Institute\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massac
 husetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and reside in the Cambridge/Bosto
 n area for the full fellowship (September through May).\nAward: USD 78\,00
 0 stipend plus an additional USD 5\,000 for project expenses. Relocation\,
  housing\, and childcare funds may also be provided. No application fee.\n
 Duration: One academic year\, September 2027 through May 2028 (residential
 )\n\nMore: https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/applicat
 ion-information?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/application-info
 rmation?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and res
 ide in the Cambridge/Boston area for the full fellowship (September throug
 h May).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloedel-reserve-creative-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative Residency at Bloedel Reserve: Class of 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bloedel Reserve's residency programme exists to foster creative
  thinking inspired by nature and to explore the connection between humans 
 and the environment. It is open to professional and emerging artists\, com
 posers\, writers\, botanists\, landscape architects\, researchers and othe
 rs working on themes of nature and human connection to it. Residents get a
  three-week stay at a 140-acre internationally recognised public garden an
 d forest preserve\, housed in a small home hidden in the woods off the mai
 n paths. Note for non-US applicants: the funding is structured as expense 
 reimbursement rather than a stipend\, capped at USD 1\,000\, which for tra
 nsatlantic applicants is unlikely to cover airfare plus meals over three w
 eeks.\n\nOrganisation: Bloedel Reserve\nLocation: Bainbridge Island\, Wash
 ington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kitsap Peninsula\, reachable by fer
 ry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap Peninsula. Residents must be 21 o
 r older. International applicants are eligible but are funded differently 
 from domestic ones (see amount).\nAward: USD 22 application fee. Furnished
  private house on the grounds with linens\, stocked kitchen\, wi-fi\, laun
 dry and use of a smart car\; an electric piano is available. Domestic resi
 dents receive a USD 1\,000 stipend. International residents receive no sti
 pend and may instead submit up to USD 1\,000 of expenses for reimbursement
 . Residents pay for their own meals and their own travel to Bainbridge Isl
 and. There is no dedicated studio\; residents work in the house.\nDuration
 : Three weeks\; residents stay one at a time with no overlap\n\nMore: http
 s://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
URL:https://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
LOCATION:Bainbridge Island\, Washington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kit
 sap Peninsula\, reachable by ferry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap P
 eninsula. Residents must be 21 or older. International applicants are elig
 ible but are funded differently from domestic ones (see amount).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:legalization-project-immigrant-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Legalization Project: Immigrant Content Creators Prog
 ram 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:The Legalization Project is running a nine-month programme for 
 experienced content creators who want to make engaging\, accessible work a
 bout U.S. immigration law\, policy and the issues affecting immigrant comm
 unities. Rather than commissioning one-off pieces\, it pairs a USD 10\,000
  stipend with actual legal grounding: training in U.S. immigration law and
  policy\, ongoing updates from immigration attorneys and advocates\, and c
 ontinuous feedback on the content you make. This suits creators already wo
 rking in video\, social or short-form documentary who want to report accur
 ately on a fast-moving legal area rather than guess at it. ELIGIBILITY: ex
 perienced content creators who are immigrants. The programme is explicitly
  open to immigrants of ALL statuses. APPLICATION: preliminary applications
  are due 17 August 2026\, through the programme page at legalizationprojec
 t.notion.site/icc-program (also circulated as bit.ly/icc-program). Being a
  preliminary application\, expect a second stage after this date.\n\nOrgan
 isation: The Legalization Project\nLocation: United States focus (content 
 covers U.S. immigration law and policy). Programme delivery format not spe
 cified in the call.\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend\, plus training in U.S. im
 migration law and policy\, regular updates from immigration attorneys and 
 advocates\, and ongoing feedback and support on your content. No applicati
 on fee.\nDuration: Nine months\n\nMore: https://legalizationproject.notion
 .site/icc-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://legalizationproject.notion.site/icc-program?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States focus (content covers U.S. immigration law and polic
 y). Programme delivery format not specified in the call.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:agci-climate-environment-data-stewardship-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260819
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:Deadline: Aspen Global Change Institute: Climate and Environment Da
 ta Stewardship Fellow
DESCRIPTION:AGCI is hiring a Climate and Environment Data Stewardship Fello
 w to coordinate and contribute to cross-organizational climate and environ
 mental data stewardship efforts\, supporting synergies and collective lear
 ning across established initiatives and networks focused on the collection
  and management of data used to inform climate action\, in ways that promo
 te long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperability and security.
  PLACEMENT AND MENTORSHIP: the fellow sits within AGCI's Engaged Research 
 Team\, reporting to AGCI's Research Director\, with day-to-day mentorship 
 from leaders at core partner organisations in the data stewardship space\,
  including the Data Foundation (Sonia Wang) and Public Environmental Data 
 Partners' co-founding member the Open Environmental Data Project (Brittany
  Janis)\, plus guidance from other leaders in the field. RESPONSIBILITIES:
  coordination of the coordinators\, using a network-of-networks approach t
 o build connectedness across emerging and existing work in the climate and
  environmental data landscape and to help the network identify and act on 
 shared priorities\; planning and coordinating network meetings\, workshops
  and other convenings\, starting with consolidating the landscape analysis
  exercises already underway\; elevating data stewardship work by developin
 g and sharing a comprehensive picture of what is needed to sustain data cr
 itical to climate action\; supporting peer learning through surveys\, focu
 s groups or scenario planning that enable cross-organisation conversations
  about possible futures\; connecting evolving data stewardship to the broa
 der climate action and investment ecosystem\; and contributing to the Clim
 ate Reserve\, including professional development programming alongside oth
 er Climate Reserve-supported fellows. REQUIREMENTS: bachelor's degree requ
 ired\, master's or doctorate preferred\; minimum 3 years managing communit
 ies of practice (working groups\, interagency groups and similar) or coord
 inating multi-organisation or network-based initiatives\, ideally related 
 to climate or environmental science or policy\; familiarity with data stew
 ardship principles (long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperabil
 ity\, security) sufficient to support conversations across technical and n
 on-technical partners\; public speaking experience\; experience working ac
 ross institutional\, sectoral and geographic contexts at the science-polic
 y interface\; strong project management\, research\, analysis and synthesi
 s skills\; and willingness to travel domestically and internationally. Dom
 ain expertise in any one data stewardship area is not required\, and knowl
 edge of the civil society data stewardship space is desirable rather than 
 expected. TERM AND PAY: full-time for one year at an anticipated USD 85\,0
 00 to 100\,000. DEADLINE: priority deadline 19 August 2026. HOW TO APPLY: 
 through the AGCI Workable posting.\n\nOrganisation: Aspen Global Change In
 stitute (AGCI)\, with Public Environmental Data Partners\nLocation: Remote
  anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's primary office is i
 n Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' approach. Travel:
  up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international trip.\nAward: Ant
 icipated salary USD 85\,000 to 100\,000\, based on the general US market.\
 nDuration: Full-time for 1 year\; start as soon as possible and no later t
 han 1 October 2026\n\nMore: https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change
 -institute/j/8C263710C9/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://apply.workable.com/aspen-global-change-institute/j/8C263710C9/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote anywhere within the continental United States. AGCI's prima
 ry office is in Basalt\, Colorado\, under an 'Institute Without Walls' app
 roach. Travel: up to 8 domestic trips a year and up to 1 international tri
 p.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-arts-in-community-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260921T042719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts in Community Grants 2027
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Arts in Community Grants fund artist fees for commu
 nity engagement projects featuring visiting Southern artists. Each funded 
 project must include both a public presentation and an educational or comm
 unity engagement component\, so this is not a straight presenting grant. U
 p to USD 10\,000 is available per project against a dollar-for-dollar cash
  match\, with a minimum artist fee of USD 4\,000 and a minimum request of 
 USD 2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: tax-exempt 501(c)(3) nonprofit arts organizations
 \, official units of local\, county or state government\, and federally re
 cognised tribal communities with nonprofit status\, located in the nine-st
 ate South Arts region (Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\
 , Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee). Applicants
  must have a Unique Entity Identifier (UEI). Applications opened 15 July 2
 026\; applicants are notified by email by 24 November 2026\, and grants ar
 e subject to funding availability. Projects must take place between 1 Janu
 ary 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline Tuesday 1 September 2026 at 11:59p
 m ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: USA\, nine-state South Arts r
 egion: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, 
 North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee.\nAward: Up to USD 10\,000 p
 er project\, covering artist fees. A dollar-for-dollar cash match is requi
 red\, so a USD 10\,000 request needs USD 10\,000 in matching funds. Minimu
 m artist fee is USD 4\,000 and the minimum grant request is USD 2\,000. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Projects must take place between 1 January 20
 27 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportun
 ities/arts-community-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
